.TH "TAKO" "1" "Jun 2026" "Tako CLI" "Tako CLI Manual"

.SH NAME
tako-heal - Inspect and control local self-healing


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtako heal [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Inspect and control node-local self-healing.

.PP
Self-healing is opt-in (selfHealing.enabled in tako.yaml). takod restarts an
unhealthy container or starts a crashed one on its own node, with backoff, a
give-up limit and a node circuit breaker. It never creates containers and
never acts during a deploy, restore or pause. See docs/SELF-HEALING.md.

.PP
Examples:
  tako heal status                       # Per-service state on every node
  tako heal status --service web         # One service with its journal
  tako heal pause                        # Pause the environment for 1h
  tako heal pause --service web --for 4h # Pause one service
  tako heal pause --until-resume         # Pause until tako heal resume
  tako heal resume                       # End pauses
  tako heal reset --service web          # Clear give-up and backoff


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for heal


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config file (default is ./tako.yaml or ./tako.json)

.PP
\fB-e\fP, \fB--env\fP=""
	environment to use (default: the only environment, else production, else the first alphabetically)

.PP
\fB--events\fP=""
	stream progress events to stdout: ndjson (human output moves to stderr)

.PP
\fB--host-key-mode\fP=""
	SSH host key verification mode: tofu, strict, ask (default: tofu)

.PP
\fB--output\fP="text"
	output format: text or json (json reserves stdout for the final result document)

.PP
\fB--preview\fP=""
	preview environment to use (deploy, ps, logs, exec, history, rollback, config explain); -e then names its base

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtako(1)\fP, \fBtako-heal-pause(1)\fP, \fBtako-heal-reset(1)\fP, \fBtako-heal-resume(1)\fP, \fBtako-heal-status(1)\fP
